  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Lifesaving Emergency Assistance for Disaster-Affected Populations in T&#xFC;rkiye&#x200B;&#x200B;</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Two earthquakes with a magnitude of 7.7 and 7.65 respectively struck Turkiye on 7 February 2023. As of 27 February, 44,374 people have lost their lives and 164,321 buildings have collapsed or are heavily damaged. There is an urgent need to provide shelter and non-food items (NFIs) to affected individuals, particularly as 1.9 million people are living in tents or temporary shelters. Thousands of people are without safe drinking water and sanitation facilities such as running water and toilets. In these conditions there is a risk of a waterborne disease outbreak. Moreover, earthquake-affected populations are exposed to compounded protection risks. Affected individuals are in need of comprehensive protection services to address ongoing vulnerabilities and preclude negative coping mechanisms. 
 
Through this project, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is contributing to efforts to save lives and alleviate suffering in the aftermath of the earthquakes that struck T&#xFC;rkiye through a multi-sectoral response, including a community-based approach to addressing the most urgent protection needs of affected populations as well as the provision of hygiene kits and items, tents and NFIs, and the deployment of the Displacement Tracking Matrix in selected sites to capture needs and inform humanitarian interventions.</narrative>
  </description>
